[Music]  la  aurora a privately run nature reserve on  the plains of eastern colombia  is home to many rare species of wild  animal  the vast grasslands are also home to  extensive cattle ranches  [Music]  jorge baragan's family has lived in the  region for generations  they own 17 000 hectares of land that's  almost  three times the size of manhattan  he's created the private la aurora  nature reserve on part of this land  because the habitats of wild animals are  shrinking fast  as forests are cleared away to create  pastures  and that's not the only problem jaguars  face here  populations across the region are at  risk of being wiped out  here on the plains jaguars kill about a  hundred carves and foals per year  most ranchers have no interest in  protecting the predatory cats  the greatest threat to their livestock  but ranchers are being asked to rethink  their approach  and to learn how to co-exist with the  big cats  in la aurora jaguars are protected and  may not be killed  baragan's nature reserve is affiliated  with the jaguar corridor initiative  run by the panthera ngo the corridor  stretches from argentina to northern  mexico  barragan collects data on the behavior  of the cats for panthera  they've installed more than 100 video  camera traps across the reserve  spots where jaguars roam which corridors  they use  we have so far identified two major  corridors in areas where they have a  better forest habitat  the one runs from east to west and the  other from north  the video recordings help them estimate  the jaguar population  and the animal's state of health if  they're doing well  it indicates that the ecosystem is in  good shape too  barragan has been observing the  movements of a certain four-year-old  female  he's named tortin he's looking for her  tracks and other traces  jaguars need large hunting grounds and  tend to move along well-established  routes  the savannah a mixed forest grassland  ecosystem  is being destroyed not just to create  pastures  but also fields for crops  nature reserves like la aurora are a  last refuge for wildlife  environment  more than 50 jaguars live here now  as well as lots of other species  um  [Music]  humans and their livestock have found a  way to coexist with the jaguars  what's more the beautiful cats are  becoming a tourist attraction  and that means a further source of  income for jorge barragan  [Music]  you

